            <ArticleTitle>Three cases illustrating the potential of dental treatment as a precipitant for weight loss leading to anorexia nervosa.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>42-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This is the first published report to illustrate the potential for dental treatment to precipitate weight loss leading to anorexia nervosa.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Documentation of history and clinical findings on three adolescents referred to a specialist eating disorder service are presented.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">These cases illustrate that dental treatment may be precipitants of weight loss leading to anorexia nervosa.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">There is a need for increased awareness of this area amongst parents of children and adolescents vulnerable to eating disorders and also their dentists.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>2006 John Wiley &amp; Sons, Ltd and Eating Disorders Association</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
